2026-07-26 23:24:36 | DEBUG    | main | [score_a9c3bc76b81f] Manual score result | {'model_id': 'manual_test_case', 'relevance': 1.0, 'coherence': 0.9745, 'completeness': 1.0, 'consistency': 0.4, 'weighted_score': 0.9036, 'confidence_tier': 'HIGH', 'latency_ms': 0.0, 'success': True, 'error': None, 'content': 'Sure! Here\'s a fun analogy to help you understand how a neural network learns:\n\n**Imagine a neural network is like a super-smart baby bird learning to fly.**\n\n1. **Starts Clueless** – The baby bird flaps its wings randomly. Some flutters are wobbly, some are too weak, and some might even crash—just like a neural network starts with random guesses.\n\n2. **Tries and Fails** – Every time the bird falls or flies badly, its brain (like a neural network) gets a little "ouch!" signal. This tells it, *"That way didn’t work—try something else!"*\n\n3. **Adjusts Tiny Muscles** – The bird’s brain doesn’t give up. It tweaks its wing movements slightly, like adjusting invisible strings, to fly a little better next time.\n\n4. **Gets Better with Practice** – After many tries, the bird finds the right balance—flapping just enough to stay in the air. The neural network does the same: it adjusts its "weights" (like invisible knobs) to make better predictions over time.\n\n5. **Becomes an Expert** – Soon, the bird soars smoothly, just like a neural network that starts making accurate guesses (like recognizing cats in photos) after lots of training.\n\n**The secret?** The more the bird (or neural network) practices, the smarter it gets—all by learning from its mistakes! ✨\n\nWould you like a real-world example to go with it? 😊\n\n### Additional Insights\n* The "Guessing" Phase (Random Weights)\nAt the very beginning, the robot chef has no idea what it’s doing.\n* You give it a bowl, some flour, and some chocolate chips.\n* It puts the "blob" in the oven, takes it out, and looks at it.\n* It’s burnt, salty, and doesn\'t look like a cookie at all.\n\n### 2.\n* Imagine you have a huge library with an infinite number of books, and each book has a secret recipe written inside.\n* The recipes are for different kinds of delicious cakes, but you don\'t know which book has which recipe.\n\nA neural network is like a very smart, magic librarian who wants to learn how to make all these cakes.\n* At first, the librarian doesn\'t know where any of the recipes are or what ingredients they need.\n* But, they have a special way of finding out.\n\nHere\'s how it works: the librarian starts by picking a book at random and trying to make the cake using the recipe inside.'}
