2026-07-26 23:36:24 | INFO     | main | [score_14d439e9fbb4] Manual score request | task_type=creative | model_id=manual_test_case | query='Give a creative analogy explaining how a neural network learns, for a 10-year-ol'
2026-07-26 23:36:32 | DEBUG    | main | [score_14d439e9fbb4] Manual score result | {'model_id': 'manual_test_case', 'relevance': 1.0, 'coherence': 0.8075, 'completeness': 0.675, 'consistency': 0.8, 'weighted_score': 0.8244, 'confidence_tier': 'MEDIUM', 'latency_ms': 0.0, 'success': True, 'error': None, 'content': 'Imagine a neural network is like a **super-smart detective** trying to solve a mystery—say, figuring out what kind of animal is in a picture.\n\nHere’s how it works:\n\n1. **The Detective Starts Clueless** – At first, the detective doesn’t know anything. They might guess "dog" for every picture, even if it’s a cat or a bird. (This is like a neural network with random guesses.)\n\n2. **They Get Hints (Feedback)** – A friend shows them the right answer: *"Nope, that’s a cat!"* The detective adjusts their thinking: *"Oh, cats have pointy ears and whiskers, not floppy ears like dogs!"*\n\n3. **Practice Makes Perfect** – The more pictures the detective sees (with the friend correcting them), the better they get. They start noticing tiny details: *"This one has stripes—must be a tiger!"*\n\n4. **The Detective’s Brain (Weights)** – Inside the detective’s brain, tiny connections (like little gears) get stronger or weaker based on what works. If guessing "dog" is wrong often, that guess becomes less likely next time.\n\n5. **Finally, They Solve the Case!** – After lots of practice, the detective can look at a new picture and say, *"That’s a parrot!"* without needing hints.\n\n**Neural Network = Detective**\n**Learning = Adjusting guesses based on mistakes**\n**Weights = The detective’s memory of what works**\n\nThe more "cases" (examples) the detective sees, the smarter they get—just like how a neural network learns from data! 🕵️♂️🔍\n\n### Additional Insights\n* Imagine you have a **giant robot chef** who wants to learn how to bake the world’s most delicious chocolate chip cookie, but he has never been in a kitchen before.\n\nHere is how he learns, step-by-step, just like a neural network:\n\n### 1.\n* The Guessing Phase (The Random Start)\nAt first, the robot chef has no idea what he’s doing.\n* He just throws ingredients into a bowl randomly—maybe a cup of salt, a whole egg with the shell on, and a scoop of dirt.\n* He puts it in the oven and pulls out a burnt, lumpy mess.\n* Imagine you have a super cool, magical library with an infinite number of books.\n* Each book has a secret message inside, but you can\'t read it yet.\n* Your job is to figure out what the message says.\n\nThe library is like a neural network, and the books are like the things it\'s trying to learn about (like pictures of cats and dogs).\n\nWhen you first start, you don\'t know what\'s in any of the books.\n* But, you have a special helper, called a "reader", that looks at the books and tries to guess what the message says.'}
